## Current Synthesis

### Idea

Give the hosted dashboard a trustworthy fleet-health view for Tool Shed releases, reporter
operation, and multiple enrolled instances. Show each instance's installed client version,
compatibility/qualification posture, last semantic report, heartbeat freshness, quiescent state,
and bounded delivery health. At project level, show the latest verified release reference,
pending-release state when safely reportable, and explicit divergence when instances disagree.

### Why It Matters

A green project badge can conceal an old client, a disabled scheduler, delayed reporting, a
quiescent worker, or two installations with different local states. The operator needs to know
whether hosted information is current and which software produced it before trusting any project
summary.

### Desired Outcome

The operator can distinguish healthy, stale, offline, quiescent, behind-version, incompatible,
unqualified, and divergent instances; see the exact source and freshness of project-level data;
and identify which local installation requires review. Project aggregation never silently merges
conflicting lifecycle snapshots. Release information clearly distinguishes installed client
version, latest known stable version, candidate/pending release state, and last production-verified
release.

## Constraints And Non-Goals

- Multiple instances are independently trusted and may legitimately differ. Preserve per-instance
  truth and compute project summaries without inventing consensus.
- Use bounded status codes, versions, timestamps, digests, and counts. Do not upload repository
  diffs, paths, raw outbox contents, credentials, scheduler definitions, or diagnostic logs.
- Canonical-version comparison must name its source and freshness; a failed external lookup must
  become `unknown`, not an assumed current result.
- Local database and release tooling remain authoritative. The dashboard cannot update, revoke,
  restart, deploy, or reconcile an instance.
- Do not expose "modified" status unless the privacy-safe meaning and evidence contract are explicit.

## Possibilities And Tradeoffs

| Possibility | Benefits | Costs / Risks | Evidence / Status |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Per-instance health cards plus project summary | Preserves source truth and makes divergence visible | More UI detail | Preferred |
| Select only the freshest instance | Simple project page | Silently hides disagreement and unhealthy instances | Reject as the only view |
| Merge reported artifact/state rows | Looks unified | Creates false authority and ambiguous conflict rules | Reject |
| Report a bounded state digest | Cheap divergence detection | Requires drilldown to explain differences | Useful companion signal |

## Open Questions

- What exact reporter-delivery indicators are privacy-safe and actionable: pending event count,
  last successful delivery, scheduler status, or only freshness/quiescence?
- Should the hosted server own canonical release lookup, or should reporters send their locally
  verified version relation and observation timestamp?
- Which pending release-cohort facts can be represented without copying local planning detail?
- What digest should detect semantic divergence while ignoring heartbeat-only differences?
- How should a project summary behave when one instance is fresh and another is intentionally
  quiescent or offline?

## Decisions

- Preserve a first-class per-instance view and never silently merge divergent local authority.
- Separate installed, canonical, candidate, and production-verified release concepts.
- Prefer controlled health fields and a semantic digest over raw diagnostic or repository data.
- Keep all repair, update, deployment, and credential actions local and governed.

## Don't Forget

- `stale`, `offline`, `quiescent`, and `delivery delayed` are different states and should not be
  collapsed into one red badge.
- Version health must account for unsupported, unqualified, locally newer, and lookup-unavailable
  states, not only "latest" versus "old".
- Reporter freshness is provenance for every other dashboard claim.

